---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Editorial policy"
  description="How educational articles are prepared, sourced, labelled, and reviewed, including the process still awaiting owner confirmation."
>
  <div class="container narrow section" data-legal-review="pending">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">How we approach a read</p><h1>
        Clear writing. Honest context.
      </h1><p>
        Our writing standards and the review work still needed before public
        launch.
      </p>
    </header>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The current status</h2>
      <p>
        The current articles are sample drafts prepared during website
        development with AI assistance. They are labelled as samples and await
        human editorial review. No clinician review, professional qualification,
        research finding, or organizational endorsement is claimed. The
        attribution “Editorial team” is used when a real author has not yet been
        supplied; it does not imply qualifications.
      </p>
      <h2>Plain language and respect</h2>
      <p>
        Use short sentences, familiar words, and explanations of new terms.
        Avoid diagnosing readers, blaming language, stereotypes, and promises
        that a habit will resolve a health condition. People have different
        circumstances and access to support. Read our <a
          href="/content-guidelines/">content guidelines</a
        > for safe messaging about sensitive subjects.
      </p>
      <h2>Sources and factual claims</h2>
      <p>
        Each article includes references for background and further reading.
        Prefer established public health resources and relevant primary sources.
        Before publication, a human editor must check that a source supports the
        claim being made, is current enough for the subject, and is represented
        fairly. A link is not an endorsement by the source.
      </p>
      <p>
        Do not invent studies, statistics, experiences, credentials, or reviews.
        Explain uncertainty and distinguish a general option from individual
        advice. If a source or claim cannot be checked, omit it or keep the
        article in review.
      </p>
      <section class="callout" aria-labelledby="editorial-process-pending">
        <h2 id="editorial-process-pending">
          Real editorial process: pending owner confirmation
        </h2>
        <p>
          This is a clearly marked process placeholder. The owner must provide
          who writes and edits articles, what qualifications each role has if
          any, how sources and sensitive topics are checked, whether any
          professional review is used, how conflicts are handled, and who
          approves publication. A planned workflow is not a completed review.
        </p>
      </section>
      <h2>Authorship and review dates</h2>
      <p>
        Show the actual author or the neutral attribution “Editorial team.” Add
        a real biography only when supplied and checked. Display published and
        updated dates separately from a last-reviewed date. Record a review date
        only after the stated review happened; otherwise say that review is
        pending. An update date alone is not evidence of clinical review.
      </p>
      <h2>Corrections and updates</h2>
      <p>
        The owner must supply a working correction contact before launch. Once
        available, readers can report a page and the issue without sending
        health information. Check factual errors, broken references, and unclear
        wording. Record substantial corrections, update the relevant date, and
        review other pages affected by the same error. The owner still needs to
        set the routine review schedule.
      </p>
      <h2>Commercial relationships</h2>
      <p>
        Advertising and affiliate features are disabled by default. Any future
        permitted commercial link or sponsorship must be clearly disclosed. It
        must not shape health claims or be presented as independent evidence.
        Get Help and articles marked sensitive must have no ads or affiliate
        recommendations. Unproven treatments, supplements, and “cures” must not
        be promoted.
      </p>
      <h2>The limits of educational reading</h2>
      <p>
        Articles do not provide personal assessment or care. See the <a
          href="/disclaimer/">medical disclaimer</a
        > for those limits, or <a href="/get-help/">Get Help</a> for routes to support.
        This policy remains a draft until the owner confirms the real process and
        completes the required review.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
